1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
Titration questions hand you a pH curve, an

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:06,000
indicator, a sample that won't dissolve, or a

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,200
conductivity graph.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,960
Each one asks: where is the equivalence point,

5
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:12,880
does the method find it, and if it

6
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,680
misses, which way does the answer move?

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,720
Here are four HSC-style questions.

8
00:00:19,934 --> 00:00:21,093
Question one.

9
00:00:21,374 --> 00:00:24,733
Twenty millilitres of ammonia solution is titrated with

10
00:00:24,814 --> 00:00:27,693
zero point one zero zero moles per litre

11
00:00:27,854 --> 00:00:31,493
hydrochloric acid, and the graph shows the pH.

12
00:00:31,854 --> 00:00:34,173
Use the graph to find the concentration of

13
00:00:34,214 --> 00:00:35,453
the ammonia.

14
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:38,013
Then select the most suitable indicator from the

15
00:00:38,094 --> 00:00:40,653
table, and justify your choice.

16
00:00:40,734 --> 00:00:42,093
Pause here to answer.

17
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:54,580
Find the equivalence point first.

18
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:56,740
It's in the middle of the steepest part

19
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:59,060
of the curve, at twenty four point zero

20
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:00,220
millilitres.

21
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,220
Moles of acid: two point four zero times

22
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:04,700
ten to the minus three.

23
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:07,860
Ammonia reacts one to one, so divide by

24
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,140
the twenty millilitre sample: zero point one two

25
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,740
zero moles per litre.

26
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:15,326
Now the indicator.

27
00:01:15,567 --> 00:01:19,086
At equivalence, the flask holds ammonium chloride, and

28
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:22,286
ammonium gives protons to water, so the pH

29
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:25,086
there is acidic, about five.

30
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:27,886
One drop either side of equivalence, the pH

31
00:01:27,967 --> 00:01:30,926
falls from about six point six to four.

32
00:01:31,007 --> 00:01:33,726
Methyl red changes between six point two and

33
00:01:33,767 --> 00:01:36,686
four point four, right inside that drop, so

34
00:01:36,767 --> 00:01:37,966
it fits best.

35
00:01:38,007 --> 00:01:39,926
Methyl orange changes at the foot of the

36
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:42,446
drop: slightly late, but acceptable.

37
00:01:43,667 --> 00:01:47,826
The classic mistake is bromothymol blue, because neutralisation

38
00:01:47,987 --> 00:01:49,826
should give pH seven.

39
00:01:49,907 --> 00:01:52,786
It starts changing about half a millilitre early,

40
00:01:52,947 --> 00:01:55,186
above the steep part, so the titre would

41
00:01:55,267 --> 00:01:56,946
come out a little too small.

42
00:01:58,167 --> 00:01:59,046
Question two.

43
00:01:59,607 --> 00:02:02,406
Another student titrates the same ammonia the other

44
00:02:02,487 --> 00:02:05,526
way round: twenty five millilitres of the zero

45
00:02:05,687 --> 00:02:08,326
point one zero zero moles per litre acid

46
00:02:08,407 --> 00:02:11,286
and phenolphthalein in the flask, and ammonia in

47
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:14,646
the burette, until the solution turns faint pink.

48
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:17,366
Explain the effect of her indicator on her

49
00:02:17,487 --> 00:02:19,726
calculated ammonia concentration.

50
00:02:20,007 --> 00:02:21,286
Pause here to answer.

51
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:33,660
Picture the curve this way round.

52
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:36,740
It starts low, near pH one, and the

53
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:40,020
equivalence point is still acidic, about five.

54
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,580
With question one's answer, that's at about twenty

55
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:44,500
point eight millilitres.

56
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:47,220
There the pH jumps, but only to about

57
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:48,020
seven.

58
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:50,740
Then the extra ammonia and the ammonium form

59
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:53,700
a buffer, so the pH creeps up slowly

60
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:55,460
instead of leaping on to ten.

61
00:02:56,667 --> 00:02:59,866
Phenolphthalein turns pink at eight point three, and

62
00:02:59,947 --> 00:03:02,106
in that buffer the pH gets there only

63
00:03:02,187 --> 00:03:05,066
after about two more millilitres of ammonia.

64
00:03:05,187 --> 00:03:07,706
So her titre is too large.

65
00:03:07,787 --> 00:03:09,706
She divides the same moles of acid by

66
00:03:09,827 --> 00:03:12,906
that bigger volume, so her ammonia concentration comes

67
00:03:13,027 --> 00:03:15,946
out too small: about zero point one zero

68
00:03:16,027 --> 00:03:18,666
eight, not zero point one two zero.

69
00:03:19,867 --> 00:03:22,586
The classic mistake is saying a bigger titre

70
00:03:22,747 --> 00:03:24,346
means a bigger concentration.

71
00:03:24,907 --> 00:03:26,746
That's true when the unknown is in the

72
00:03:26,827 --> 00:03:27,466
flask.

73
00:03:27,947 --> 00:03:30,426
Here the unknown is in the burette, so

74
00:03:30,507 --> 00:03:32,266
its volume goes on the bottom.

75
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:34,266
Question three.

76
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:37,786
Zero point two zero zero grams of crushed

77
00:03:37,867 --> 00:03:41,066
eggshell reacts with twenty five millilitres of zero

78
00:03:41,227 --> 00:03:43,786
point two five zero zero moles per litre

79
00:03:43,947 --> 00:03:46,506
hydrochloric acid, an excess.

80
00:03:46,827 --> 00:03:49,866
After boiling, the leftover acid needs twenty four

81
00:03:50,027 --> 00:03:52,986
point nine zero millilitres of zero point one

82
00:03:53,227 --> 00:03:56,506
zero zero zero moles per litre sodium hydroxide.

83
00:03:57,147 --> 00:04:00,106
Find the percentage of calcium carbonate, and explain

84
00:04:00,187 --> 00:04:02,346
the effect of a burette rinsed only with

85
00:04:02,507 --> 00:04:02,906
water.

86
00:04:03,547 --> 00:04:04,826
Pause here to answer.

87
00:04:15,234 --> 00:04:17,513
The four back titration steps.

88
00:04:17,794 --> 00:04:20,913
Added: six point two five zero times ten

89
00:04:20,994 --> 00:04:22,753
to the minus three moles.

90
00:04:22,994 --> 00:04:26,433
Left over, equal to the sodium hydroxide: two

91
00:04:26,594 --> 00:04:28,753
point four nine zero times ten to the

92
00:04:28,834 --> 00:04:29,873
minus three.

93
00:04:30,194 --> 00:04:33,793
Subtract: the eggshell used three point seven six

94
00:04:33,874 --> 00:04:36,113
zero times ten to the minus three.

95
00:04:36,434 --> 00:04:38,833
Halve it for the one to two ratio:

96
00:04:38,914 --> 00:04:41,153
one point eight eight zero times ten to

97
00:04:41,234 --> 00:04:43,633
the minus three moles of calcium carbonate.

98
00:04:44,114 --> 00:04:47,033
That's zero point one eight eight two grams,

99
00:04:47,154 --> 00:04:50,193
out of zero point two zero zero: ninety

100
00:04:50,274 --> 00:04:51,713
four point one per cent.

101
00:04:52,934 --> 00:04:54,053
Now the burette.

102
00:04:54,534 --> 00:04:57,973
Water left inside dilutes the sodium hydroxide, so

103
00:04:58,014 --> 00:04:59,893
it takes more of it to neutralise the

104
00:04:59,974 --> 00:05:01,013
leftover acid.

105
00:05:01,494 --> 00:05:03,893
The titre is too big, so the leftover

106
00:05:04,054 --> 00:05:06,613
acid looks too big, the acid used looks

107
00:05:06,694 --> 00:05:09,653
too small, and the percentage of calcium carbonate

108
00:05:09,774 --> 00:05:10,853
comes out too low.

109
00:05:11,574 --> 00:05:14,293
The fix: rinse the burette with the solution

110
00:05:14,334 --> 00:05:15,253
it will deliver.

111
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:19,106
The classic mistake is thinking a bigger titre

112
00:05:19,267 --> 00:05:21,506
means more calcium carbonate.

113
00:05:21,547 --> 00:05:24,226
In a back titration, the titre measures what's

114
00:05:24,387 --> 00:05:25,426
left over.

115
00:05:25,627 --> 00:05:28,226
So more left over means less sample.

116
00:05:29,434 --> 00:05:30,873
Question four.

117
00:05:30,954 --> 00:05:34,553
Fifty millilitres of blue-dyed ammonia is titrated with

118
00:05:34,634 --> 00:05:37,513
zero point five zero zero moles per litre

119
00:05:37,674 --> 00:05:40,873
hydrochloric acid, and the graph shows the corrected

120
00:05:41,074 --> 00:05:42,313
conductivity.

121
00:05:42,394 --> 00:05:44,713
Explain its shape, and use it to find

122
00:05:44,754 --> 00:05:46,713
the ammonia's concentration.

123
00:05:46,874 --> 00:05:48,073
Pause here to answer.

124
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:01,140
Ammonia is a weak base, so it's mostly

125
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:05,220
whole molecules, with few ions: the conductivity starts

126
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:05,700
low.

127
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:09,220
Each portion of acid turns ammonia into ammonium

128
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:12,740
ions and adds chloride ions, so ions build

129
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:15,660
up steadily, and the graph rises gently.

130
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:19,140
After equivalence there's almost no ammonia left, so

131
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,340
the extra acid adds hydronium, which conducts far

132
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:24,980
better, and the graph climbs steeply.

133
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:29,360
Equivalence is where the two straight stretches meet.

134
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:32,200
Draw a best-fit line through each, and they

135
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:35,400
cross at eight point four zero millilitres, between

136
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:36,640
two readings.

137
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,880
Moles of acid: four point two zero times

138
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:42,120
ten to the minus three, one to one

139
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:43,120
with ammonia.

140
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:46,600
Divide by the fifty millilitre sample: zero point

141
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:49,320
zero eight four zero moles per litre.

142
00:06:50,534 --> 00:06:53,413
The classic mistake is reading equivalence at nine

143
00:06:53,494 --> 00:06:56,773
millilitres, the first reading on the steep part.

144
00:06:56,894 --> 00:07:00,053
That gives zero point zero nine zero zero,

145
00:07:00,134 --> 00:07:01,253
too high.

146
00:07:01,334 --> 00:07:04,613
Equivalence usually falls between readings, so use the

147
00:07:04,694 --> 00:07:05,413
lines.

148
00:07:06,634 --> 00:07:07,833
A pH curve?

149
00:07:08,314 --> 00:07:10,393
Equivalence is in the middle of the steepest

150
00:07:10,474 --> 00:07:13,433
part, and the indicator must change inside it.

151
00:07:13,994 --> 00:07:14,553
An error?

152
00:07:15,034 --> 00:07:17,833
Decide which way the titre moves, then follow

153
00:07:17,914 --> 00:07:19,113
it through the formula.

154
00:07:19,354 --> 00:07:22,073
In a back titration, more left over means

155
00:07:22,154 --> 00:07:23,233
less sample.

156
00:07:23,514 --> 00:07:24,953
A conductivity graph?

157
00:07:25,194 --> 00:07:27,193
Read where the lines cross.

158
00:07:27,674 --> 00:07:29,993
Next, how to choose the right method for

159
00:07:30,074 --> 00:07:30,873
a sample.

